  "query_preview": "period of strong westerly flow aloft that will persist \ninto early next week despite dominant ridging building for the \nfirst half of the weekend. The trend of the strongest winds aloft \nstaying north of the Canadian border on Friday continues to gain \ntraction, but a tightening surface pressure gradient and mountain \nwave activity still looks to enhance stronger winds along the \nRocky Mountain Front Friday into early Saturday. \n\nEnsembles are favoring the ridge flattening and sending a fast\nmoving trough into the forecast area Sunday into Monday. This \nwill bring another round of winds, strongest along the Rocky \nMountain Front, and some more light precipitation along the \nnorthern divide. Temperatures look to remain above average this \nweekend into early next week, with only a slight cool down \nfollowing any shortwave passages. - RCG\n\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\n\u2026Periods of stronger westerly winds Thursday through early next week\u2026\n\nNBM probabilistic guidance continues to highlight Friday into\nSaturday  for the strongest winds, with probabilities for 55 mph \nwind gusts running 70 to 90% along the Rocky Mountain Front and \nthe plains along and west of highway 89 and along and west of I15 \nover Glacier County. The 40 to 60% chance for 75 mph wind gusts \nremains confined to the immediate eastern slopes of the Northern \nRocky Mountain Front and will be heavily reliant on mountain wave \nactivity. The plains east of I15 also look windy during this time \nas well, especially the central MT highway 87/200 corridor where \n30 to 50 mph gusts will be common. The winds for Sunday into \nMonday have slightly lower probabilities despite stronger winds \naloft, this looks to be mostly a trough timing issue. - RCG",
